    description: "Phase 6 PR3 (commit ea0154e54f) drove the `Symbol.stable_id` collision rate from 60.2% to 0.8% by adding `name` and `qualified_name` to the hash inputs at `analyze/base.py::compute_stable_id` (the untyped-tier factory used by bash/ruby/scala/many extended1 analyzers) and at `py.py::_compute_stable_id`. INV-bazij P0 was closed structurally.\n\nTwo residual collision categories remain (~0.8% of the corpus, 289 collisions across 95 groups):\n\n**(a) Typed-tier `make_typed_stable_id` callers without name input.** Several mainstream analyzers (go, csharp, java, kotlin, php, rust, swift, scala, dart, js_ts) use `analyze/base.py::make_typed_stable_id(kind, normalized_signature, visibility)` — same-signature siblings (e.g., overloaded method `foo(int)` and `foo(string)` in the same class) still hash identically because the factory contract has signature as the disambiguator. Phase 6 PR3 fixed py.py by prepending qualified-name into normalized_signature; the same fix needs to land in the other 10 analyzers' callers.\n\n**(b) Linker-emitted call_site collisions.** Framework-pattern outputs (kafka:publish, http call_sites, etc.) collide on duplicate logical addresses — e.g., two `kafka.publish(\"topic-a\", ...)` calls in different files produce two Symbols with the same `stable_id` because the factory contract intentionally collapses them. Whether this is a bug or a feature depends on the consumer: cross-run identity (the original stable_id purpose) wants collision; per-emit-site identity wants distinction. Needs a design decision, then either expand the factory's hash inputs to include the source file/line OR document the policy.\n\nImplementation order: (a) is mechanical and fits one batched PR. (b) needs a tracker discussion to settle the design question first.\n\nReferences: ADR-0014 §\"Typed stable_id factories\"; Phase 6 PR3 commit ea0154e54f deferred-items."  # 5175
